             while after the .sdf had been moved below it, so a regeneration
             would have put the lidar back on the roof without a word.

             UNDER_DRONE mount: 0.15 m below base_link, as on the aircraft.

             INVERTED — roll pi. Position unchanged; only the direction it
             looks has flipped. The Mid-360's field of view is -7 to +52
             degrees about its own x-y plane, i.e. mostly UPWARD, so hanging it
             under the airframe the right way up points that cone at the sky.
             Measured consequences of doing exactly that, on 2026-09-03:

               on the pad   only 4 of the 32 vertical rings point downward at
                            all, so the whole scene is a ring of ground at
                            2.4 m radius. 1440 of 11520 rays return.
               at 15 m      a ground return inside the 40 m range needs 20.6
                            degrees of depression. The sensor has 7. Ground is
                            122 m away, and it sees nothing but structures
                            TALLER than the aircraft within 40 m.

             That is survivable for "do not hit a building taller than me",
             which is all the costmap was ever asked for, and it leaves a
             lidar-inertial estimator with no geometry at all: FAST-LIO2
             drifted 118 m in 40 s with the aircraft parked on the pad.

             Inverted, the cone points down, -52 to +7 degrees, and the ground
             is 11.7 m away at transit height.

             mount_z and mount_roll in
             vision_landing/launch/livox_mid360.launch.py MUST match this, and
             so must the real aircraft. Together they are the claim "the
